"The configured sampler is deterministic under seed 12345, so I'll extend the analysis with quantile, ESS, and exact-reference computations, then run the full configured pipeline end-to-end:## Posterior Inference for θ — Final Report\n\n**Run:** 4 chains × 2000 sampling iterations (2000 warmup discarded each) = **8000 retained draws**, seed 12345. Deterministic and reproducible.\n\n### Posterior Summaries\n\n| Quantity | MCMC estimate | Exact Beta(9, 5) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Posterior mean** | **0.647** | 0.643 |\n| **Posterior sd** | **0.122** | 0.124 |\n| **95% credible interval** | **[0.401, 0.864]** | [0.386, 0.861] |\n| Median | 0.656 | 0.650 |\n\n### Convergence Diagnostics\n\n| Diagnostic | Value | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **R̂ (split)** | **1.0015** | ✅ < 1.01 — converged; chains agree from overdispersed starts |\n| **ESS (multi-chain, Geyer)** | **1846** | ✅ ~23% efficiency; far above the ~400-draw adequacy rule of thumb |\n| MCSE of mean | 0.0029 | mean accurate to ~±0.006 (2×MCSE) |\n| Acceptance rate | 0.43–0.47 | at the 0.44 target |\n\n### Interpretation\n\n- **Estimate:** Given y = 7/10 with a Beta(2, 2) prior, θ has posterior mean ≈ **0.65** with sd ≈ 0.12 — the data pulled the prior mean (0.5) upward, as expected.\n- **Uncertainty:** The 95% credible interval **[0.40, 0.86]** is wide — unavoidable with n = 10. It excludes neither 0.5 nor the data proportion 0.7.\n- **Sampler quality:** R̂ = 1.0015 and ESS ≈ 1850 mean the estimates are trustworthy. Every MCMC figure lands within Monte Carlo error of the exact conjugate answer (mean is off by 1.4 × MCSE; the 2.5% quantile shows slightly more wiggle, which is normal — tail quantiles are the noisiest summaries).\n- Because this model is conjugate, the exact Beta(9, 5) column doubles as a correctness proof of the sampler — the MCMC machinery recovers the analytic posterior.\n\nArtifacts in the workspace: `mcmc_posterior.py` (sampler + diagnostics config), `posterior_summary.py` (summaries/ESS/R̂), `prior_predictive.py` (step 2)."